{"item":{"aggregated_output":"{\"exit_code\":0,\"stderr\":\"\",\"stdout\":\"Total output lines: 210\\n\\nTotal output lines: 231\\n\\n 100: \\r\\n 101: // I’ve gone back and forth between whether to sort case-sensitively or\\r\\n 102: // insensitively by default. The default string sort in most programming\\r\\n 103: // languages takes each character’s ASCII value into account, sorting\\r\\n 104: // “Documents” before “apps”, but there’s usually an option to ignore\\r\\n 105: // characters’ case, putting “apps” before “Documents”.\\r\\n 106: //\\r\\n 107: // The argument for following case is that it’s easy to forget whether an item\\r\\n 108: // begins with an uppercase or lowercase letter and end up having to scan both\\r\\n 109: // the uppercase and lowercase sub-lists to find the item you want. If you\\r\\n 110: // happen to pick the sublist it’s not in, it looks like it’s missing, which\\r\\n 111: // is worse than if you just take longer to find it.\\r\\n 112: // (https://ux.stackexchange.com/a/79266)\\r\\n 113: //\\r\\n 114: // The argument for ignoring case is that it makes exa sort files differently\\r\\n 115: // from shells. A user would expect a directory’s files to be in the same\\r\\n 116: // order if they used “exa ~/directory” or “exa ~/directory/*”, but exa sorts\\r\\n 117: // them in the first case, and the shell in the second case, so they wouldn’t\\r\\n 118: // be exactly the same if exa does something non-conventional.\\r\\n 119: //\\r\\n 120: // However, exa already sorts files differently: it uses natural sorting from\\r\\n 121: // the natord crate, sorting the string “2” before “10” because the number’s\\r\\n 122: // smaller, because that’s usually what the user expects to happen. Users will\\r\\n 123: // name their files with numbers expecting them to be treated like numbers,\\r\\n 124: // rather than lists of numeric characters.\\r\\n 125: //\\r\\n 126: // In the same way, users will name their files with letters expecting the\\r\\n 127: // order of the letters to matter, rather than each letter’s character’s ASCII\\r\\n 128: // value. So exa breaks from tradition and ignores case while sorting:\\r\\n 129: // “apps” first, then “Documents”.\\r\\n 130: //\\r\\n 131: // You can get the old behaviour back by sorting with `--sort=Name`.\\r\\n 132: impl Default for SortField {\\r\\n 133:     fn default() -> Self {\\r\\n 134:         Self::Name(SortCase::AaBbCc)\\r\\n 135:     }\\r\\n 136: }\\r\\n 137: \\r\\n 138: impl DotFilter {\\r\\n 139:     /// Determines the dot filter based on how many `--all` options were\\r\\n 140:     /// given: one will show dotfiles, but two will show `.` and `..` too.\\r\\n 141:     /// --almost-all is equivalent to --all, included for compatibility with\\r\\n 142:     /// `ls -A`.\\r\\n 143:     ///\\r\\n 144:     /// It also checks for the `--tree` option, because of a special case\\r\\n 145:     /// where `--tree --all --all` won’t work: listing the parent directory\\r\\n 146:     /// in tree mode would loop onto itself!\\r\\n 147:     ///\\r\\n 148:     /// `--almost-all` binds stronger than multiple `--all` as we currently do not take the order\\r\\n 149:     /// of arguments into account and it is the safer option (does not clash with `--tree`)\\r\\n 150:     pub fn deduce(matches: &MatchedFlags<'_>) -> Result<Self, OptionsError> {\\r\\n 151:         let all_count = matches.count(&flags::ALL);\\r\\n 152:         let has_almost_all = matches.has(&flags::ALMOST_ALL)?;\\r\\n 153: \\r\\n 154:         match (all_count, has_almost_all) {\\r\\n 155:             (0, false) => Ok(Self::JustFiles),\\r\\n 156: \\r\\n 157:             // either a single --all or at least one --almost-all is given\\r\\n 158:             (1, _) | (0, true) => Ok(Self::Dotfiles),\\r\\n 159:             // more than one --all\\r\\n 160:             (c, _) => {\\r\\n 161:                 if matches.count(&flags::TREE) > 0 {\\r\\n 162:                     Err(OptionsError::TreeAllAll)\\r\\n 163:                 } else if matches.is_strict() && c > 2 {\\r\\n 164:                     Err(OptionsError::Conflict(&flags::ALL, &flags::ALL))\\r\\n 165:                 } else {\\r\\n 166:                     Ok(Self::DotfilesAndDots)\\r\\n 167:                 }\\r\\n 168:             }\\r\\n 169:         }\\r\\n 170:     }\\r\\n 171: }\\r\\n 172: \\r\\n 173: impl IgnorePatterns {\\r\\n 174:     /// Determines the set of glob patterns to use based on the\\r\\n 175:     /// `--ignore-glob` argument’s value. This is a list of strings\\r\\n 176:     /// separated by pipe (`|`) characters, given in any order.\\r\\n 177:     pub fn deduce(matches: &MatchedFlags<'_>) -> Result<Self, OptionsError> {\\r\\n 178:         // If there are no inputs, we return a set of patterns that doesn’t\\r\\n 179:         // match anything, rather than, say, `None`.\\r\\n 180:         let Some(inputs) = matches.get(&flags::IGNORE_GLOB)? else {\\r\\n 181:             return Ok(Self::empty());\\r\\n 182:         };\\r\\n 183: \\r\\n 184:         // Awkwardly, though, a glob pattern can be invalid, and we need to\\r\\n 185:         // deal with invalid patterns somehow.\\r\\n 186:         let (patterns, mut errors) = Self::parse_from_iter(inputs.to_string_lossy().split('|'));\\r\\n 187: \\r\\n 188:         // It can actually return more than one glob error,\\r\\n 189:         // but we only use one. (TODO)\\r\\n 190:         match errors.pop() {\\r\\n 191:             Some(e) => Err(e.int...55 characters truncated...stricts:expr => $result:expr) => {\\r\\n 216:             #[test]\\r\\n 217:             fn $name() {\\r\\n 218:                 use crate::options::parser::Arg;\\r\\n 219:                 use crate::options::test::parse_for_test;\\r\\n 220:                 use crate::options::test::Strictnesses::*;\\r\\n 221: \\r\\n 222:                 static TEST_ARGS: &[&Arg] = &[\\r\\n 223:                     &flags::SORT,\\r\\n 224:                     &flags::ALL,\\r\\n 225:                     &flags: